What are the three? Monks, in this world, a warrior can shoot far, is a sharp-shooter, and breaks through a large body. Monks, a warrior possessed of these three qualities is worthy of a king, is an asset to a king, and is reckoned as a limb of the king. Even so, monks, a monk possessed of three qualities is worthy of offerings... is an unsurpassed field of merit for the world. What are the three? Monks, in this world, a monk can shoot far, is a sharp-shooter, and breaks through a large body.

And how, monks, does a monk shoot far? Monks, in this teaching, whatever form—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near—a monk sees all form as it really is with correct wisdom: 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.' Whatever feeling—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near—a monk sees all feeling as it really is with correct wisdom: 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.' Whatever perception—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near—a monk sees all perception as it really is with correct wisdom: 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.' Whatever volitional formations—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near—a monk sees all volitional formations as they really are with correct wisdom: 'These are not mine, these I am not, these are not my self.' Whatever consciousness—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near—a monk sees all consciousness as it really is with correct wisdom: 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.' In this way, monks, a monk shoots far.

And how, monks, is a monk a sharp-shooter? Monks, in this teaching, a monk understands as it really is: 'This is suffering'; he understands as it really is: 'This is the origin of suffering'; he understands as it really is: 'This is the cessation of suffering';
